WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.088
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.088 --> 00:00:07.573
<v Examiner>Define health literacy, and describe strategies a nurse can use to educate a patient with

3
00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.839
<v Examiner>low health literacy, including the teach-back method.

4
00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:14.867
Personal health literacy is a person's ability to find,

5
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:20.527
understand and use information and services to make health decisions for themselves and

6
00:00:20.527 --> 00:00:26.251
others: to navigate the system, understand their condition, manage long-term conditions,

7
00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:29.309
use medicines safely and give informed consent.

8
00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:34.773
Organisational health literacy is how equitably an organisation makes that possible.

9
00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:36.920
And the line from Janine's slide:

10
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.823
health literacy is not determined by intelligence or income.

11
00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:42.185
Why it matters.

12
00:00:42.185 --> 00:00:46.197
People with poor health literacy are less likely to use screening,

13
00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:48.872
less likely to manage a long-term condition,

14
00:00:48.872 --> 00:00:53.309
and more likely to be admitted to hospital and to use emergency services.

15
00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:58.719
New Zealand is the only country with a framework that makes health literacy core business

16
00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:00.239
across the health system.

17
00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:01.838
Now the strategies.

18
00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:05.890
The gold standard is teach-back, in three steps: Ask, Build, Check.

19
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.091
Ask what they already know, for example, what do you already know about your diabetes?

20
00:01:11.091 --> 00:01:13.328
Build on it with the new information.

21
00:01:13.328 --> 00:01:18.408
Then Check they actually understood, not by asking do you understand, but by saying,

22
00:01:18.408 --> 00:01:20.827
tell me and show me what you understand.

23
00:01:21.277 --> 00:01:26.731
Alongside it: arrange a professional interpreter, and use multilingual resources.

24
00:01:26.731 --> 00:01:29.761
Use plain language, or explain medical terms.

25
00:01:29.761 --> 00:01:34.271
Limit it to three to five key points, and be specific, not general.

26
00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:37.570
Demonstrate, draw pictures, use models or videos.

27
00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.994
Be positive, hopeful and empowering.

28
00:01:39.994 --> 00:01:44.370
And set up the learning environment: quiet, private, comfortable.

29
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.083
For a child, play therapy works, like a six-year-old with an I V line.

30
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:54.991
Health literacy: the ability to find, understand and use health information and services;

31
00:01:54.991 --> 00:01:57.015
not about intelligence or income.

32
00:01:57.015 --> 00:02:00.143
Low literacy means less screening, more admissions.

33
00:02:00.143 --> 00:02:03.270
Teach-back is the gold standard: Ask, Build, Check,

34
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.662
"tell and show me what you understand".

35
00:02:05.662 --> 00:02:10.568
Interpreter, plain language, three to five key points, demonstrate, be positive.
